      "content": "1|\n2|> *“No permanent party, no permanent problem.”*\n3|\n4|[![TC_The Adventurers Guildhall Night Characters_Interior Scene.jpg](https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/62750da488ff573e9bdc63c4/097ccf1b-7b44-4efd-8718-28b4da94edae/TC_The+Adventurers+Guildhall+Night+Characters_Interior+Scene.jpg)](https://www.tomcartos.com/adventurers-guildhall?itemId=tngvf4gvej5buyqrb5arpgscoykqsx)\n5|\n6|The **FLS Guildhall** is the informal headquarters of the [[Free League of Stickyickistan]], a loud, practical, semi-organized adventuring hub where rotating crews gather between jobs. It is less a noble institution and more a shared clubhouse, contract office, message board, tavern backroom, records archive, and monster-hunter break room all crammed under one roof.\n7|\n8|The guildhall serves the open-table nature of the Free League: adventurers come and go, parties form around posted jobs, DMs introduce new threats, and the setting grows through whatever survives contact with the players.\n9|\n10|## Overview\n11|\n12|**Type:** Adventurers’ guildhall / contract office / public tavern-adjacent headquarters  \n13|**Location:** A grey brick road near the merchant docks on the east side of [[Nevercough Island]], [[Stickyickistan]]  \n14|**Nearby:** [[Greywake Docks]]  \n15|**Affiliation:** [[Free League of Stickyickistan]]  \n16|**Known For:** Rotating parties, public contracts, monster hunts, shared rumors, and questionable paperwork  \n17|**Primary Notice Boards:** [[The Rolling Papers]] and [[The Roach Clip]]  \n18|\n19|---\n20|\n21|## Description\n22|\n23|The FLS Guildhall is always busy, but rarely organized. The front room smells like rain-soaked cloaks, old paper, smoke, cheap coffee, spilled malt, floor wax, and whatever monster part someone swore was “fully preserved.” Adventurers crowd the tables between jobs, comparing scars, arguing over maps, sharpening blades, filling out forms badly, and pretending not to listen when someone else mentions a better-paying contract.\n24|\n25|Maps of [[Mythmos]], [[Stickyickistan]], and distant trouble spots cover the walls, many of them pinned with colored string, bounty slips, wax seals, and handwritten corrections from adventurers who survived the trip. Public contracts, civic emergencies, missing-person notices, and monster attacks circulate openly through weekly copies of [[The Rolling Papers]]. Less official opportunities drift through the room in copies of [[The Roach Clip]], whispered referrals, folded notes, and conversations that become quieter when clerks walk past.\n26|\n27|The guildhall does not make the Free League tidy. It makes the chaos legible. The hall exists so dangerous people can find dangerous work in a place with witnesses, paperwork, rumors, and at least a thin chance of accountability before the next disaster gets worse.\n28|\n29|---\n30|\n31|## What Adventurers Can Do Here\n32|\n33|- Find public adventure postings from [[The Rolling Papers]]\n34|- Hear about discreet or illegal jobs from [[The Roach Clip]]\n35|- Form a party for a one-shot or short arc\n36|- Meet DMs, patrons, handlers, and faction contacts\n37|- Research monsters, regions, and old adventure reports\n38|- Buy basic adventuring supplies\n39|- Arrange downtime, spellcasting services, or recovery help\n40|- Report completed contracts and collect payment\n41|- Display trophies, maps, warnings, and “do not touch this” items\n42|\n43|---\n44|\n45|## Important Areas\n46|\n47|### The Main Board\n48|\n49|The most visible board in the guildhall, covered in public contracts, monster warnings, missing-person notices, festival security jobs, and civic emergencies. Most jobs here are legal, public, and appropriate for adventurers who want to be paid without explaining themselves to a magistrate.\n50|\n51|### Weekly Papers\n52|\n53|Every week, fresh copies of [[The Rolling Papers]] and [[The Roach Clip]] make their way through the guildhall. The Rolling Papers are stacked openly near the Main Board, passed around over breakfast, and clipped to public contract files. The Roach Clip is less official, but just as common: folded into coat pockets, tucked between map pages, or quietly handed from one adventurer to another.\n54|\n55|No one needs a special shady corner to find work. Public jobs come through headlines, notices, and proper paperwork. Questionable jobs circulate through coded classifieds, rumors, dead-drop instructions, and people who know how to read between the lines.\n56|\n57|The guildhall does not endorse illegal work, anonymous bounties, or anything that says “do not involve the authorities,” but it also does not inspect every paper an adventurer brings inside. As long as no one causes trouble in the hall itself, the clerks let the weekly editions float around and let adventurers make their own bad decisions.\n58|\n59|### The Trophy Wall\n60|\n61|A chaotic display of monster parts, broken weapons, signed sketches, fake plaques, and real warnings. Any trophy mounted here is assumed to have a story, a smell, or a curse attached.\n62|\n63|### The Records Nook\n64|\n65|A cramped archive of past adventure reports, character records, faction notes, monster sightings, and DM scribbles. The filing system is technically alphabetical if you ask the one person who understands it.\n66|\n67|---\n68|\n69|## Services\n70|\n71|Guildhall services improve with a character’s Free League tier. Higher-tier adventurers have better reputations, better paperwork, and access to clerks, handlers, suppliers, and specialists who do not waste time on unproven rookies.\n72|\n73|### Standard Services\n74|\n75|| Service | Cost | Available To | Notes |\n76||---|---:|---|---|\n77|| Basic job registration | Free | All tiers | Lets a party claim a public contract |\n78|| Contract filing | Free / small fee | All tiers | Records who accepted what and who survived |\n79|| Basic supplies | At cost | All tiers | Rope, rations, torches, chalk, oil, common gear |\n80|| Weekly papers | Free | All tiers | Access to circulating copies of [[The Rolling Papers]] and [[The Roach Clip]] |",
